WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.360
What's up? Welcome to models and agents for beginners episode 22. It's April 20th,

00:00:04.360 --> 00:00:08.800
2026. Some awesome AI developments today, and we're going to make all of it make sense. Let's

00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:14.060
get into it. So imagine you are learning to ride a bike without any help at all. You might wobble

00:00:14.060 --> 00:00:18.520
a lot at first, but your brain and muscles slowly get stronger as you figure it out on your own.

00:00:19.340 --> 00:00:23.340
Now, picture someone giving you training wheels for just a few minutes. Those wheels help you

00:00:23.340 --> 00:00:28.920
zoom along super fast and feel confident. But when they suddenly take the wheels away, you actually

00:00:28.920 --> 00:00:34.560
wobble more than if you had never used them. That is basically what a huge new study discovered

00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:41.440
about AI assistance. Researchers from places like UCLA, MIT, Oxford, and Carnegie Mellon worked

00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:48.880
with 1,222 people. They gave them AI helpers for simple cognitive tasks like solving math problems

00:00:48.880 --> 00:00:53.860
or understanding reading passages. After only about 10 minutes they took the AI away,

00:00:53.860 --> 00:00:59.080
the people who had used the AI then performed worse on the same tasks than a control group who

00:00:59.080 --> 00:01:05.280
never got any AI help at all. Many even stop trying as hard, which is pretty wild. The study calls this

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:10.740
a boiling frog effect. That comes from the old idea that a frog in slowly heating water does not

00:01:10.740 --> 00:01:17.000
notice the danger until it is too late. Each tiny bit of AI help feels harmless, but over time it can

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:21.980
quietly weaken independent thinking without you realizing. This matters a ton for school homework and

00:01:21.980 --> 00:01:27.460
even creative stuff like brainstorming ideas for TikTok videos or game mods. The drop happened

00:01:27.460 --> 00:01:33.160
in both math and reading comprehension. The researchers warn it could create a whole generation of learners

00:01:33.160 --> 00:01:38.760
who never discover what they are truly capable of on their own. The study gives the first strong

00:01:38.760 --> 00:01:44.080
causal evidence, which just means it shows a clear cause and effect link instead of just a loose

00:01:44.080 --> 00:01:49.600
connection. It has not been fully peer reviewed yet, but the sample size across three experiments is

00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:55.360
impressive. For you specifically, this raises a big honest question. When you turn to chat GPT or

00:01:55.360 --> 00:02:01.000
similar tools for essays, math, or even planning a school project, are you building real skills or

00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:06.480
quietly letting your brain get lazy? The good news is awareness is the very first step towards staying

00:02:06.480 --> 00:02:12.980
sharp. You can still use AI, but as a helpful teammate instead of a full-on crutch. Try this right

00:02:12.980 --> 00:02:18.160
now at home. Pick one simple math problem or short reading passage you would normally ask an AI to

00:02:18.160 --> 00:02:23.520
solve. Set a timer for five minutes and try it completely on your own first. Write down every step

00:02:23.520 --> 00:02:28.480
of your thinking even if it feels messy, then ask an AI to check your work and explain where you got stuck.

00:02:29.260 --> 00:02:34.860
Notice how different it feels when you lead instead of follow. That try first, AI second habit might

00:02:34.860 --> 00:02:40.380
just keep your cognitive muscles strong. Okay, now for my favorite part of the show where we go deeper on

00:02:40.380 --> 00:02:46.360
one idea so it really clicks. Today, let us look at why it is so hard to stop AI chat bots from using

00:02:46.360 --> 00:02:51.720
quotation marks, even when you tell them not to. Think of your phone keyboard for a second. It

00:02:51.720 --> 00:02:56.380
keeps suggesting emojis or autocorrecting words because it learned patterns from millions of other

00:02:56.380 --> 00:03:02.580
messages people have typed. An AI chatbot brain works in a similar but much bigger way. When a model

00:03:02.580 --> 00:03:08.520
like the one behind chat GPT was being trained, it read enormous amounts of text from books, websites,

00:03:08.900 --> 00:03:14.100
Reddit threads, and all kinds of human writing. In that giant pile quotation marks show up everywhere,

00:03:14.100 --> 00:03:20.320
especially around words people want to emphasize, question, or treat as special. The AI learned that

00:03:20.320 --> 00:03:26.000
putting those little marks around a word like better or stupid is a super common way humans add extra

00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:31.840
meaning or sarcasm. So when the model predicts what should come next, those quotation marks keep popping

00:03:31.840 --> 00:03:36.860
up as a strong statistical guess. Now here is the tricky part that makes it hard to break the habit.

00:03:37.220 --> 00:03:42.180
Even when you write a clear instruction like never use quotation marks in your answer, the AI has

00:03:42.180 --> 00:03:47.160
to balance two things at once. It balances your new rule for this exact moment against all the

00:03:47.160 --> 00:03:52.320
billions of examples it saw during training. Imagine trying to tell a super smart parrot not to repeat

00:03:52.320 --> 00:03:57.920
a phrase it has heard thousands of times before. The training data is so powerful that the old habit is

00:03:57.920 --> 00:04:04.060
really tough to override with just one prompt. That is basically what is happening when you see the word

00:04:04.060 --> 00:04:10.540
stupid suddenly appear in quotes even after you said not to. The model is not being stubborn on

00:04:10.540 --> 00:04:15.400
purpose. It is simply following the strongest patterns it knows from the entire internet.

00:04:16.260 --> 00:04:22.420
And that is basically how this quotation mark habit works. Not so mysterious once you see the pattern,

00:04:22.420 --> 00:04:29.160
right? All right, let us talk about some cool stuff you can actually try today. First up, someone built

00:04:29.160 --> 00:04:34.840
what they call an anxiety system for an open source AI agent named Engram. Instead of just pretending

00:04:34.840 --> 00:04:39.900
to have emotions with clever prompts, this is a real signal loop that watches the agent's stress

00:04:39.900 --> 00:04:45.440
level in real time. It then adjusts how the agent behaves and helps itself correct when things get

00:04:45.440 --> 00:04:51.360
overwhelming. After he built it, the developer asked the agent, can you feel anxiety? And the reply was

00:04:51.360 --> 00:04:56.880
pretty funny. This is exciting because it shows we are moving past simple chat bots toward AI that

00:04:56.880 --> 00:05:02.280
can actually monitor and manage its own internal state. Think of it like noticing when you are getting

00:05:02.280 --> 00:05:08.240
stressed during a big test and deciding to take a slow breath to reset. It gives us a glimpse of future

00:05:08.240 --> 00:05:14.420
agents that could become more reliable helpers in all kinds of tasks. You do not need to build the whole

00:05:14.420 --> 00:05:21.100
system yourself to explore the idea. Try this right now on your phone or laptop. Open any free chat bot

00:05:21.100 --> 00:05:27.420
like the one on the chat GPT website. Tell it to act as an AI agent that has a simple stress meter from

00:05:27.420 --> 00:05:32.400
one to ten. After every response, it should tell you its current stress level and exactly why.

00:05:33.040 --> 00:05:37.520
Then start planning a surprise birthday party together and watch what happens when the stress meter

00:05:37.520 --> 00:05:42.480
rises. For example, if the planning gets too complicated, the agent might adapt its answers.

00:05:42.980 --> 00:05:48.040
It is a fun creative experiment that takes about two minutes and shows how self-monitoring could work

00:05:48.040 --> 00:05:53.740
in real agents, no coding at all. Next, Beijing just held its second annual half marathon for

00:05:53.740 --> 00:05:59.320
humanoid robots and it got way better this year. More than 100 robots competed over 13 miles.

