The winner was a red robot named Lightning made by the smartphone company, Honour. It finished in only

00:06:05.300 --> 00:06:10.820
50 minutes and 26 seconds, which actually beat the recent human world record for that distance.

00:06:11.380 --> 00:06:16.820
About 40% of the robots ran completely on their own without any remote control. Last year,

00:06:16.880 --> 00:06:22.580
the robots were slow, clumsy and needed tons of human help. This year showed real progress and

00:06:22.580 --> 00:06:27.880
balance, speed and autonomy, even though some still fell over. It makes you start imagining a

00:06:27.880 --> 00:06:33.040
future where robots could help with delivery routes, dangerous exploration or even fun sports.

00:06:33.520 --> 00:06:38.520
Try this on your phone right now. Open the YouTube app and search for Beijing robot half marathon

00:06:39.320 --> 00:06:45.060
2026 or Honour Lightning robot race. Watch the highlights, especially the winner crossing the finish line.

00:06:45.420 --> 00:06:50.760
Then check out the funny crash moments too. Ask yourself which parts look like the robot is deciding

00:06:50.760 --> 00:06:56.060
on its own versus being remote controlled. It is a great way to see how fast robot bodies are

00:06:56.060 --> 00:07:01.660
improving and it might spark ideas about what robots could do in your lifetime. Time for a few quick

00:07:01.660 --> 00:07:07.120
bits to round out the show. New reports suggest chat GPT is no longer dominating the AI chatbot space

00:07:07.120 --> 00:07:11.840
quite as strongly as it once did. Other models are catching up fast in areas that matter most

00:07:11.840 --> 00:07:17.400
to everyday users like you and me. It is a good reminder that this whole AI world changes quickly,

00:07:17.400 --> 00:07:22.860
so what feels hottest today might have strong competition tomorrow. A recent piece from the BBC

